  <!-- TECH CAREER -->
  <section id="tech-career">
    <h2>Tech Career</h2>
    <p>Since you wanted more, you get the long version.</p>
    <p>I've worked in the tech industry since 2012.</p>
    <p>My first corporate job was a product internship at the mobile gaming company GREE. I worked on a game called Dino Life and learned gamification tactics (people will pay a lot for a rainbow stegosaurus).</p>
    <p>Post-college I joined Guidebook (no-code mobile apps for events) as employee #26. I launched our higher-ed vertical, then our London-based EMEA operations, as a Customer Success Associate. Then I originated the Product Marketing function, working on sales enablement and strategy, before shifting into Product Management.</p>
    <p>I was at Guidebook for 4.5+ years, and I consider that where I "grew up" in tech: a truly scrappy environment, in the rose-tinted 2010s sense of that word. Late nights with Thai food, flipcup at office parties, calling the CTO when our site crashed, soul-crushing product rollouts, heroic acts of service from beloved teammates, and learning every function from scratch.</p>
    <p>I needed a change, so I applied to business school. Because I was in SF, a lot of people told me an MBA was useless. I was also sheepish because I knew it would make me <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=XfB0g_JDIds" target="_blank">Jared from Silicon Valley</a>, but I <em>did</em> ultimately want it, for three reasons:</p>
    <ul class="about-list">
      <li>To get better at business strategy. Like, I legitimately wanted to learn more about the nuts and bolts of business and break down how companies <em>work</em>.</li>
      <li>To effectively hold my own against executives and founders. Despite working closely with C-suite, I had impostor syndrome around them. I wanted to fix that.</li>
      <li>To meet people outside of the Silicon Valley Bubble.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>I got rejected. I tried again a year later, and was accepted into Harvard Business School for fall 2018. HBS was great. I had a better time than expected. If you want to know more about my experience there, <a href="https://lithub.com/mfa-vs-mba-finding-unlikely-literary-inspiration-at-harvard-business-school/" target="_blank">here's an essay on why I chose an MBA over an MFA in Creative Writing</a>.</p>
    <p>In summer 2019 I interned at Duolingo in Pittsburgh, where I worked on Monetization, specifically the question: "Why do people upgrade to Plus?" One of my features (Hard Mode) drove +$2.5k in daily revenue during the experiment phase. I enjoyed Duo's design-forward, experiment-driven culture, and applying the knowledge from my mobile game days. Alas, I also really, really wanted to move to New York.</p>
    <p>In 2020 I graduated, then joined the seed-stage start-up Thrilling (Etsy but for vintage clothing, which was crucial during covid). I was the third technical hire, after our CTO and one fullstack dev. I'd describe my role there as 50% Product, 50% Operations/whatever the CEO needed. This involved maturing our tech stack (implementing Zoom, Lever, Intercom, analytics, etc), and hiring. (A <em>lot</em> of hiring.) It was eye-opening to be part of the Series A fundraise, and to build a team from scratch. But part of me really wanted to join a later-stage org, and see how I'd fare in that environment.</p>
    <p>In 2021 I joined Klaviyo's Data Infrastructure team, working on Profiles and Segmentation. My primary focus was on improving our segment builder. Over time, my team and I converted the UI to React/our design system; added in segment preview, cloning, additional operators, support for multiple where clauses; and fully upgraded the underlying infrastructure, drastically improving speed for our largest accounts, and decreasing overall costs.</p>
    <p>I also worked on AI/ML features, launching our RFM segments and dashboard, before expanding my coverage in Data Infra. In the last 2 years I worked across the Data Platform (Data Warehouse/Data Lake/Online Analytics), supporting infra teams with a focus on enabling flexible queries for analytics features.</p>
    <p>Klaviyo was the first place where I worked on teams that were entirely backend-focused. It was fun and fulfilling to drive technical decisions for foundational parts of our app, and collaborate closely with our (mostly internal) customers. There's more I want to learn in this area, so I'm spending part of my sabbatical studying how data-intensive applications are made.</p>
  </section>

  <!-- ME AS A PRODUCT MANAGER -->
  <section id="product-manager">
    <h2>Me as a Product Manager</h2>
    <p>I believe the core of the PM role boils down to the following:</p>
    <ul class="about-list">
      <li>Deeply understand your customers, their problems, and what makes them happy.</li>
      <li>Unblock your teams, so they can solve customer problems as effectively as possible within your context (limited resources, company politics, etc).</li>
    </ul>
    <p>A lot of the value I add as a PM focuses on those two areas. I'm happiest when I'm building actual solutions. There are periods when the job becomes <em>mostly</em> management/comms. I can do it, but I get kinda grumpy if it goes on too long.</p>
    <p>Where I spike as a PM:</p>
    <ul class="about-list">
      <li>Customer research and interactions. I use my Customer Success/Product Marketing background heavily. I'm great at running my own interviews and drawing insights to inform things like ICP, product decisions, and roadmaps.</li>
      <li>Communication. I'm comfortable presenting, and can talk across functions, roles, levels.</li>
      <li>Documentation. I take a lot of pride in my note-taking (yes, even in our AI world), and writing stuff down so that work doesn't slip through the cracks, and decisions don't shift between meetings.</li>
      <li>Collaborating with engineering. You will not need to argue with me on the importance of paying down technical debt or refactoring key components. I too want to build stable, functional products, with codebases you're happy to work in. I too want thoughtful rollouts and the ability to iterate. My favorite dev/PM working relationships are built on mutual trust where we seek the best solution, together.</li>
      <li>Design. I care about the details. I care about how things <em>look.</em> I can make my own wireframes/mock-ups, and I enjoy the design and iteration process.</li>
      <li>Figuring out whatever's needed to get things done.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>I also think polish separates good PMs from really great ones: an attention to detail and quality, that elevates everything they touch. I hope to be that for the teams I work on.</p>
  </section>

  <!-- ARE YOU LOOKING FOR A JOB? -->
  <section id="looking-for-job">
    <h2>Are you looking for a job?</h2>
    <p>I'm looking to start full-time employment again in Dec 2026/Jan 2027.</p>
    <p>That said, if you have an interesting opportunity—let's chat! I'm selectively looking for consulting or part-time projects to work on through 2026. Or if you think a role will be available for me in Q1 of next year, I'm happy to start the conversation early.</p>
    <p><strong>My preferences:</strong></p>
    <ul class="about-list">
      <li>NY-based or remote</li>
      <li>I like being an IC, but I'd be open to management if the need is there.</li>
      <li>Series C onwards</li>
      <li>Industry agnostic. I have a slight preference for consumer, but I did spend 4+ years working on infra, so I'm open.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>People are the most important thing. I would love to work again with people I've worked with previously (assuming, of course, that the feeling is mutual).</p>
    <p>Core values I look for in a team's culture: integrity, ownership, kindness, customer-focus, creativity.</p>
    <p><strong>Consulting/Part-time Projects I can help with:</strong></p>
    <ul class="about-list">
      <li>User research to answer specific product questions or develop an ICP/buyer persona</li>
      <li>Roadmap development</li>
      <li>E2E product or feature development (taking a fuzzy idea to something built and deployed)</li>
      <li>Understanding the role of product in your company. If you're in the transition from "founders doing product" to "now we need a dedicated PM," I can help clarify the functions, and provide advice on whether you actually <em>need</em> to hire.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <!-- OKAY ENOUGH ABOUT TECH -->
  <section id="other-interests">
    <h2>Okay enough about tech, tell me some other interesting things</h2>
    <ul class="about-list">
      <li>I'm a writer, and have been publishing fiction since 2009.
        <ul class="about-list">
          <li>I have a published short story collection, and you can read a lot of my <a href="https://isabelyap.com/fiction" target="_blank">fiction for free online</a>. I also write poetry. I'm currently working on a novel.</li>
          <li>I also teach writing through workshops, lectures, and school visits. I have a <a href="https://visyap.mymagic.page/" target="_blank">free monthly newsletter</a> about my writing practice and advice.</li>
        </ul>
      </li>
      <li>If you're curious about how/what I write, some representative works:
        <ul class="about-list">
          <li><a href="http://www.tor.com/2015/08/12/milagroso-isabel-yap/" target="_blank">Near-future sci-fi about synthetic foods</a> (or <a href="https://podcasts.apple.com/us/podcast/milagroso-by-isabel-yap/id1244649384?i=1000528691472" target="_blank">listen to LeVar Burton read it</a>)</li>
          <li><a href="https://www.lightspeedmagazine.com/fiction/windrose-in-scarlet/" target="_blank">Red Riding Hood and Beauty falling in love</a></li>
          <li><a href="http://strangehorizons.com/fiction/asphalt-river-mother-child/" target="_blank">Underworld goddess x the Philippines' bloody drug war</a></li>
        </ul>
      </li>
      <li>I love reading. Visiting a bookstore is one of my favorite ways to spend a weekend afternoon.</li>
      <li>I love music. Playlists I've made include greasy songs to drive to, guitar cry, k-pop, OPM, and motown.
        <ul class="about-list">
          <li>I <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=SCCNhdNKNKM" target="_blank">play the ukulele</a> and sing.</li>
          <li>At HBS I <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Xvo91OyoXDs" target="_blank">sang alto</a> for our acapella group, HOTS.</li>
          <li>I learned piano growing up but am severely out of practice.</li>
        </ul>
      </li>
      <li>I studied Japanese from sixth grade through college, including a language-intensive semester in Tokyo as part of my Japanese Studies minor. I've passed the JLPT N2. I'm rusty, but conversational. (I've forgotten most of my kanji though.)
        <ul class="about-list">
          <li>I've traveled different parts of Japan, including chasing summer festivals in Northern Tohoku, farming in rural Kyoto, and hiking the Kumano Kodo.</li>
          <li>This fall, I'll be walking the Shikoku Henro.</li>
        </ul>
      </li>
      <li>I love learning things. As a kid I learned paper quilling, crocheting, knitting, juggling, solving the Rubik's cube one specific way, and painting with oils/acrylics/pastels.
        <ul class="about-list">
          <li>Things I want to learn someday: philosophy, more foreign languages (improve my Japanese, learn Korean and Spanish), watercolors, guitar, drawing, tarot cards, music theory, how to get better at learning.</li>
        </ul>
      </li>
      <li>I'm bad at basically all sports. But one of my sabbatical goals is to develop healthier habits, including consuming more protein (I say this to be funny, but I'm not joking).</li>
      <li>I have a meditation practice, and regularly take classes with Elizabeth Pyjov at <a href="https://www.happinessangha.com/" target="_blank">Happiness Sangha</a>.</li>
      <li>I suspect part of my comfort in tech comes from being a nerd. I like playing videogames and watching anime.
        <ul class="about-list">
          <li>Everyone should watch Nippon Sangoku.</li>
          <li>Everyone should play Blue Prince.</li>
        </ul>
      </li>
      <li>Other hobbies: walking around the city, visiting museums, looking at beautiful things in nature, having good conversations with friends.</li>
      <li>I am afraid of the sound of birds' wings flapping.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

</Layout>

<style>
